  "body": "The maintainer reported this in chat on 2026-11-20:\n\n> Most machinists define their WCS at the center of a part or at a corner in a mill. This doesn't mean that the cutter starts from that WCS point, it just means that it references it. The fault would be that the cutter moves to the starting point as a first move. It doesn't have to. This is a general post-processing problem and operations calculation issue. The machine should move to its first starting point for an operation, in relation to the WCS, not start at the WCS. It doesn't matter where the WCS is. The first operation starts where it has to start to clear the part, but in relation to the WCS.\n\nWhat the maintainer sees: the first move of an operation goes to the WCS origin (X0 Y0). It does not go to the start point that the operation needs.\n\nExpected:\n- The WCS is only the reference of the coordinates. It is not a point that the tool must visit.\n- The first positioning move of each operation goes to the real start point of that operation: the entry position that clears the part and the stock.\n- That point is written in coordinates of the WCS of the setup. It is correct for any WCS position (part center, corner, or elsewhere).\n\nWhy P1: a first move to a wrong point can move the tool into the part, the stock or the fixture at rapid. This is wrong output (Prime Directive).\n\nNot known yet. The research answers these questions in comments:\n- Where the defect shows: the toolpath in the viewport, the simulation, the CL file, the posted G-code, or several of these.\n- If it is in the operation calculation (the first point of the toolpath), in the post (the initial move or the safe-start block), or in both.\n- If it depends on the operation type, the entry type, the machine file, or the post dialect.\n- If it is the same fault as the retired #2382 (coordinates measured from the world origin), or a different one. #2382 was about the origin of the coordinates. This issue is about the first move target.\n- Which test or fixture proves it: a part with its WCS at the center and a part with its WCS at a corner, with the same operation.\n\nDone means: a pinning test per operation family shows that the first move goes to the entry point of the operation in WCS coordinates, and not to X0 Y0, for a WCS at the center and at a corner. The research comment lists the files and the sub-issues.\n\nRelated: #2382 (closed).\n",
